<h2>Operational Pipeline</h2>
<p>
  Every encoding job follows a six-stage pipeline that separates media inspection and argument planning from child-process execution:
</p>

{{ signalPipeline({
  title: "SIX-STAGE SIGNAL ROUTING PIPELINE",
  stages: [
    {
      step: "STAGE 01",
      owner: "FluxEncoder.Core",
      name: "Resolve & Validate Inputs",
      detail: "Expand files, directories, or remote:// aliases; enforce path and scheme policies before touching media.",
      tag: "BatchPlanner / SourcePolicy"
    },
    {
      step: "STAGE 02",
      owner: "FFprobe",
      name: "Probe Source Streams",
      detail: "Inspect container, codecs, dimensions, duration, HDR metadata, and frame rate via FFprobe JSON output.",
      tag: "FFprobeMediaProbe"
    },
    {
      step: "STAGE 03",
      owner: "FluxEncoder.Core",
      name: "Verify Hardware & Plan Args",
      detail: "Probe hardware encoders (NVENC, AMF, QSV, VideoToolbox) with a test encode and assemble ArgumentList tokens.",
      tag: "EncodePlanBuilder"
    },
    {
      step: "STAGE 04",
      owner: "FluxEncoder.Cli",
      name: "Confirm Execution Plan",
      detail: "Present source-to-target stream mappings or emit structured dry-run JSON without modifying files.",
      tag: "--dry-run / --json"
    },
    {
      step: "STAGE 05",
      owner: "FFmpeg",
      name: "Execute & Sample Telemetry",
      detail: "Write to a hidden .partial file beside the target while parsing -progress pipe:1 and process counters.",
      tag: "FFmpegRunner"
    },
    {
      step: "STAGE 06",
      owner: "FluxEncoder.Infrastructure",
      name: "Verify & Commit Output",
      detail: "Validate non-empty output size, atomically promote .partial to final path, and commit run telemetry.",
      tag: "SqliteHistoryStore"
    }
  ]
}) }}

<h2>Terminal Telemetry Architecture</h2>
<p>
  In interactive UTF-8 terminals, FluxEncoder renders a fixed-width dashboard using standard box-drawing characters (<code>┌</code>, <code>├</code>, <code>│</code>) and block glyphs (<code>█</code>, <code>░</code>). Column padding is calculated on visible grapheme width prior to wrapping strings in ANSI escape sequences so right-hand borders remain locked at column 80 regardless of encoder name length.
</p>

{{ tuiPreview({
  command: "flux encode --input raw_footage.mkv --hardware nvidia --codec hevc --quality balanced",
  caption: "Figure 1: Flux Core Matrix 80-column layout. Non-interactive sessions, pipes, and --json automatically bypass ANSI cursor movement."
}) }}

